bviously vital that we honor the pledge  that was made during the 2019 uh general  election campaign that we wouldn't raise  at that tax and that's something which  uh the chancellor has confirmed will now  be scrapped with effect from the 6th of  November that increase in in tax that's  worth 330 pounds for 28 million people  but he's obviously going to go much  further than that and he's going to  tackle uh what is a record high tax  burden on on families and and businesses  reflecting clearly the fact we've gone  through some extraordinarily difficult  years but setting out a fundamentally  new approach to go for growth to make  sure that we we win the argument that a  more successful enterprise economy is  good for the whole of this country and  it's that which we need to unlock today  well if you're confident that this plan  will work and growth will be boosted why  has the office for Budget responsibility  not been asked to produce its  independent analysis of what it means  for the economy it would normally go  alongside statements like this well the  obr will be producing a a full forecast  later in the financial year now well  precisely because as they themselves has  said that given this is a short notice  event they would not be able to complete  the usual quality of analysis that they  would offer and so they will be asked to  conduct that modeling alongside uh  subsequent events later in this  financial year this is uh an attempt to  to kick-start growth it's delivered at  short notice it's a great speed and  we've been working on it a huge rapidity  over recent days but it is not something  which the obr can model uh to their  usual to their usual standards but they  will be they will be doing so in due  course but but are you concerned of the  effect that could have on the market is  the pound for example has taken a hit  recently don't the markets need to see  what these policies will do for the  economy and whether we can afford them  what the markets want to know is whether  the UK economy is going to grow and that  is something which the chancellor is  setting are the absolute heart of our  strategy that the UK wants to uh to get  back to those levels of trend growth  that we used to enjoy and critically  catch up with other parts of the world  where Dynamic economies are surging  ahead the risk is that Europe is in a a  pattern of stagnation Britain can't  afford to be part of that and we need to  to set out today how we are going to uh  get to get to a much higher level of  sustainable growth and that's that's  going to be driven through lowering the  burden of tax well if corporation tax if  the rise in corporation tax is scrap  then businesses will be very happy Liz  truss has acknowledged that the  scrapping of the rise in National  Insurance will benefit the wealthier  more labor says that this government has  moved from leveling up to to trickle  down you're leveling up secretary are  you worried about this government being  seen to be helping or helping the better  off more than the poorer people uh  absolutely not this is I mean this whole  term trickle down is such a nonsense and  is itself a uh a center-left  mischaracterization of what this  government is all about we need to grow  the economy because a more successful  economy is good for everybody if we have  a more successful economy then the tax  state goes up that tax is what funds our  Vital Public Services it is a virtuous  circle as the chancellor will be saying  later today we need to get to a  situation where the UK economy is  stronger is more Dynamic is more robust  that in turn makes it easier to pay for  the services on which the poorest and  the most vulnerable in our society rely  and is leveling up secretary I'm  absolutely delighted that we're going to  be going for growth going for new  policies like investment zones which  will benefit some of the the most Left  Behind communities in our country and  help to unlock uh growth o