oductive outputs we will be making  a statement today which is about  actually unlocking the productive  sectors of the economy if you get if you  if you are creating the conditions for  stronger underlying growth through tax  cuts that's a very different proposition  to just spending uh for spending sake  which is what the opposition I'm afraid  uh remain committed to doing we are  trying to unlock better Trend growth and  that puts obviously the whole question  of how you pay for public services on a  fundamentally different footing you're  talking about a very different economic  proposition and a very frankly bold and  welcome switch to an open Embrace of  growth that's the main the main task of  the ex-jacker  we're told that part of the plans are  these local investment zones in 38 areas  in England which involve amongst other  things liberalizing local planning to to  release more loud for housing and  Commercial development so how much say  will local residents have in the  planning and the building of this  infrastructure in their area well  investment zones are a really important  part of the chancellors uh statement and  I should I shouldn't preempt that but  what I what I can say is that these  zones will only happen where there is  local consent and we've been very clear  about that in the discussions we've been  having uh with local authorities and  Mayors over recent days as we've been  testing the proposition with them  there's I'm delighted to say there's  been a huge level of enthusiasm in those  discussions and uh we hope to be able to  make very rapid progress over the next  few weeks in terms of confirming where  these zones will be located but to be  clear they will only happen where there  is a local appetite for them to occur  there will be no top down in position of  these zones we want to obviously create  the best possible opportunity uh for for  local areas to to get both targeted tax  breaks and a liberalized planning regime  and that's 